{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJEB59114","title":"Microbial communities in coagulant-treated sewage sludge","description":"Anaerobic digestion (AD) and composting are commonly utilized sludge management methods however, the influence of different coagulant types on these biological processes and their stabilized biomass characteristics have not been fully explained. In this study, the effect of the coagulant used in municipal wastewater treatment on the biological stabilization of sludge was investigated. Fully controlled and monitored small-scale AD and composting bioreactors were utilized. The coagulants tested included an inorganic coagulant (IC), polyaluminium chloride (PAC), and organic coagulants, (OC) polyamine (pAmine) and chitosan (Chit). Overall, the coagulant applied showed a measurable influence on the biological stabilization of sludge. It was found that the presence of complex organics compounds from OC in the sludge biomass decreased biomass biodegradability but increased gas production. During AD, Chit-sludge achieved higher biogas production than pAmine- and PAC-sludges (13% and 16%, respectively, in Nm3 CH4 t-1VS). In composting, pAmine-sludge achieved the highest feedstock temperature (34-35℃) and CO2 gas emissions, followed by Chit- (33℃) and PAC-sludges (32℃). Generally, tot-P concentration in PAC-sludge was higher than in pAmine and Chit-sludges both before (20, 17 and 15 g/kg DM, consecutively) and after AD (23, 21 and 20.5 g/kg DM, consecutively), and during the composting (31, 29.5 and 26 g/kg DM, consecutively) process. Tot-N concentrations (g/kg DM) showed a substantial increase after AD (pAmine and PAC ca 50% and Chit 81%), while a decrease was observed after composting, specifically in PAC-sludge (PAC 28%, pAmine and Chit ⁓5%). The selection of the most suitable coagulant by wastewater treatment facilities depends on the objective of the biological stabilization process. In cases where AD is applied and biogas yield is selected as the target output, the semi-natural OC Chit was found to be the best option among the coagulants tested. Comparably, when the nutrient content of resulting biosolids (AD or composting) is more relevant, it was found that OC-produced sludge contained higher N concentrations, while IC-produced sludge contained slightly higher P concentrations.","organism":null,"data_type":"Other","center_name":"Natural Resources Institute Finland","submission_date":"2023/01/23 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","city","lat","lon","urban_status","urban_context","eligibility_reason","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","host","location_resolution","country_iso2","place_id","location_status","location_method","location_note","geo_loc_raw","collection_year"],"samples":[["SAMEA112356166","pAmine_MidComp_1","2020-08-15","Finland",null,null,null,null,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","urban context stated at BioProject level only",null,null,null,null,null,"country","FI",null,"unresolved",null,null,"Finland",2020],["SAMEA112356167","Chit_MidComp","2020-08-15","Finland",null,null,null,null,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","urban context stated at BioProject level only",null,null,null,null,null,"country","FI",null,"unresolved",null,null,"Finland",2020],["SAMEA112356168","pAmine_MidComp_2","2020-08-15","Finland",null,null,null,null,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","urban context stated at BioProject level only",null,null,null,null,null,"country","FI",null,"unresolved",null,null,"Finland",2020],["SAMEA112356169","PAC_MidComp","2020-08-08","Finland",null,null,null,null,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","urban context stated at BioProject level only",null,null,null,null,null,"country","FI",null,"unresolved",null,null,"Finland",2020],["SAMEA112356170","Chit_FinComp","2020-11-06","Finland",null,null,null,null,"probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","urban context stated at BioPro